{"id":"e9bf6d63-4630-4719-9a0e-585902b80fec","object":"chat.completion","created":1793331402,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Reading environ.py header to check imports/circularity before editing.\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", 1, 30)[\"content\"])\nprint(\"---\")\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\", 1, 32)[\"content\"])\n```","reasoning_content":"Now I have all needed mechanisms. Implement envs config, logger formatter, stats extra.\n\nEnviron: add DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(...) reading env, None if unset, int >=32 else ValueError containing name (descriptor wraps errors with name anyway, but spec says error text must contain var name — descriptor wraps. But for safety include name in message).\n\nlogger: formatter truncation. Need to compute message after substitution before prefix. Approach: in format, before super().format, compute the message text via record.getMessage() — but careful not to mutate record. Actually super().format sets record.message. We can compute message ourselves and truncate, but need not mutate shared record. Options: temporarily set record.message = truncated and restore... but spec says cannot rewrite record.msg/args; record.message is temporary. Simpler: compute truncated message and produce output manually? Better: call super().format(record) which sets record.message = getMessage(). If record has keep_full_message not True and max_message_chars is not None, we need to truncate the message portion while keeping prefix, exception text, stack_info.\n\nApproach: temporarily set record.message to truncated value, call super().format(record), then restore? But super().format uses record.message to compute output — for the format string \"%(message)s\" it uses record.message. Exception text appended after record.message. So setting record.message temporarily works. However spec says cannot rewrite msg/args/exc_info/stack_info — record.message is allowed but must restore. Alternatively wrap: save old message, set truncated, format, restore.\n\nBut careful: super().format(record) itself sets record.message = record.getMessage() at start (in logging.Formatter.format: record.message = record.getMessage()). So pre-setting won't survive. Let's check CPython: Formatter.format does:\n```\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = self.formatTime(...)\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ...\n```\nSo we need to intercept formatMessage. Override formatMessage: format the message ourselves truncated. Because format string with %(message)s uses record.message. We can in format(): set record.message via getMessage, then temporarily modify... Actually simplest: override format to do:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg_text = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._truncate(record, msg_text)\n    if truncated is not msg_text:\n        # temporarily override getMessage? \n```\n\nCleanest: temporarily set record.message and format via copying record? Use logging.makeRecord copy? But changes identity issues; tests may check shared record preserved.\n\nTrick: temporarily patch record.getMessage to lambda: truncated? That's hacky but works: save record.getMessage, set to lambda, call super().format, restore. Actually Formatter.format calls record.getMessage(). We can do:\n\n```\nold_get = record.getMessage\nrecord.getMessage = lambda: truncated  # type: ignore\ntry:\n    msg = super().format(record)\nfinally:\n    del record.getMessage  # restore\n```\nHmm, assigning attribute on record; getMessage is a bound method from class. Setting instance attribute shadows. del removes it. But restore with setattr(record, 'getMessage', old_get)? That leaves instance attribute equal to bound method — fine but persists. Better del then falls back to class. But if record already had instance attribute (unlikely), preserve. Use try/except.\n\nAlternatively, avoid touching record: build output manually replicating Formatter.format including exception handling. That's error-prone but straightforward:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message held...\n```\nHmm, formatMessage uses self._style.format(record) which needs record.message. We can set record.message ourselves (Formatter does it anyway), then compute s = self.formatMessage(record) — but message in there is full. To truncate we could instead do:\n\n```\nmessage = record.getMessage()\ntruncated = truncate(message)\nif truncated != message:\n    # use a shallow copy of record for formatting\n    import copy\n    rec = copy.copy(record)\n    rec.message = truncated\n```\nBut copy of LogRecord via copy.copy is fine and formatter sets rec.message = rec.getMessage() again overriding. So also need to patch getMessage on copy: rec.getMessage = lambda: truncated. Then super().format(rec). Since rec is a copy, exc_info/stack_info same objects; exception formatting works. But `record.message` check `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message` uses record.message (full). Should use the effective message for newline handling. Keep using truncated.\n\nBetter approach: compute truncated, then temporarily set record.message and override getMessage, then restore. copy.copy is cleaner and doesn't touch shared record at all. But LogRecord may contain args etc. copy.copy shallow -> `rec.args` same tuple, getMessage overridden. Exception text appended. asctime computed. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: tests may check that formatter output's prefix uses correct values — copy preserves.\n\nBut careful: after logging, handlers may rely on record.message? We don't mutate original.\n\nLet's implement:\n\n```\n_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _coerce_max(max_message_chars):\n    if max_message_chars is None: return None\n    if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    return max_message_chars\n```\n\nTruncate function:\n```\ndef _truncate_message(message, budget):\n    if len(message) <= budget: return message\n    remaining = budget - len(_OMITTED)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nBudget 32: remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. Matches.\n\nformat method:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = record.getMessage()\n    max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n    truncated = _truncate... if budget and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True\n```\nNote: `keep_full_message` exactly True — use `record.__dict__.get(\"keep_full_message\") is True` or getattr is True. But `keep_full_message=1` -> 1 is not True since `1 is True` False. Good.\n\nThen:\n```\nif truncated is msg:\n    text = super().format(record)\nelse:\n    rec = copy.copy(record)\n    rec.getMessage = lambda: truncated\n    text = super().format(rec)\n```\nWait super().format(rec) sets rec.message = rec.getMessage() = truncated. formatMessage uses message. Also uses `record.exc_text` caching — copy gets exc_text from original if already computed? Original record may have exc_text set from a previous handler; copy shares string, fine.\n\nBut `record.message` in newline logic: use effective message = truncated.\n\nImplement newlines:\n```\nif \"\\n\" in text and truncated:\n    prefix = text[: text.find(truncated)]\n    text = text.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n```\nHmm careful: if truncated message not in text due to formatting alternatives? format string is \"%(message)s\" default. But if custom fmt, message appears. Original code used record.message. To be safe, use record.message? But truncated and record.message full: text.find(full message) would fail. Use effective = truncated. If find returns -1, prefix = text[:-1] weird. Guard: idx = text.find(effective); if idx >= 0. Keep original behaviour otherwise.\n\nActually simpler: keep `if \"\\n\" in text and effective:` and compute prefix only if found; else prefix = \"\". Original assumed found.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```\n        effective = rec.message if ... \n```\nUse variable `effective_message`.\n\nThen color and return.\n\nAlso need `import copy` or use logging.makeRecord? Use `copy` module — allowed import in action code limited but this is source file; fine to import any stdlib in the file.\n\nNow _setup_logger: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Import: logger.py currently imports `from deepinfer.logger import ...`? logger.py doesn't import environ. Add `from deepinfer import environ`? Check environ imports logger? Let me check environ.py head lines 1-30. I read earlier? Pending. Need check for circular import. environ.py probably imports logging maybe not logger. Let's read head.\n\nAlso note `rearm` example: cached value 32 then change env to 64: reinit reads cached 32.\n\nEnviron var definition: need None when unset, int>=32:\n```\nDEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_log_max_message_chars)\n```\nwith helper function or lambda. Also \"可增加必要标准库辅助函数\" and descriptor factory method allowed. I'll add a staticmethod `int_or_none_min` ? Simpler: define module-level function `_max_message_chars(v)`.\n\nError message must contain var name: descriptor wraps type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") — ValueError gets name prefixed. But if raised in factory, descriptor wraps it. So include name anyway? Spec: \"错误文字须包含变量名\". Descriptor ensures that. But if the value is validated at format time? no. For safety include variable name in our message too: raise ValueError(\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: must be an integer >= 32, got ...\").\n\nBut then descriptor wraps -> \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\" acceptable.\n\nCareful: int(v) with v=\"\" raises ValueError. int accepts \"  32  \" and \"+32\" and underscores? int(\"1_0\") works in Python? int(\"1_0\") = 10 yes. Fine.\n\nAlso int(v) where v=\"0x20\" fails. fine.\n\nShould